     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Substance-exposed newborns:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " under federal CAPTA, state policies must require health care providers involved in delivering or caring for an infant affected by prenatal drug exposure, withdrawal or fetal alcohol spectrum disorder to notify child protective services."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Plan of safe care:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " CAPTA also calls for a plan of safe care for these infants after they leave the hospital, addressing the health and substance use disorder treatment needs of the infant and the family or caregiver."
     }
    ]
   }
